### 1. Overall Sentiment

The dominant sentiment around Software Engineering (SWE) as a career today is **cautiously optimistic but noticeably anxious**, and often **fragmented by seniority and employment status**. In professional circles, there’s sustained confidence that software remains foundational to most industries, and there’s real excitement about how AI tooling can remove tedious work and speed delivery. At the same time, a persistent undercurrent of anxiety shows up in conversations about hiring slowdowns, tougher entry-level pipelines, and whether “coding” is becoming less of the job’s center of gravity. ([The JetBrains Blog][1])

Public commentary tends to amplify **replacement narratives** (“AI will replace programmers”), while working engineers more often express a **pragmatic mixed stance**: “AI is powerful, but it’s unreliable enough that accountability still sits with humans.” This gap between public hype and practitioner caution contributes to a sentiment that feels less like a unified consensus and more like a split between **opportunity framing** and **threat framing**. ([Stack Overflow][2])

A useful way to summarize the emotional tone: **optimism about tools, realism about the market, and anxiety about how the career ladder works in an AI-saturated environment**. ([The JetBrains Blog][1])

